<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wexford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wexford is a port town in south-eastern Ireland at the mouth of the River Slaney. It grew from a Norse settlement of the 9th or 10th century, and in 1169 it became the first town in Ireland captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a>, who had been invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, King of Leinster. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s army stormed and sacked it in 1649.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wexford.jpg" alt="Wexford" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wexford_-_aerial_-_2024-08-31_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wexford is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a>, on the south side of Wexford Harbour, the estuary of the River Slaney near the south-eastern corner of Ireland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> named it Veisafjǫrðr, meaning inlet of the mudflats, which became Weiseforthe in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> dialect of Middle English and then Wexford. Its Irish name, Loch Garman, comes according to a story in the dindsenchas from Garman mac Bomma Licce, who stole the queen's crown from Temair during the feast of Samhain, was chased to the river mouth and drowned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 9th to the early 12th century Wexford was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> town, largely independent and owing only token dues to the Irish kings of Leinster. In May 1169 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Dermot MacMurrough</a> and his Norman ally Robert Fitz-Stephen besieged it; the Norse inhabitants resisted fiercely until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a> persuaded them to accept a settlement. Afterwards it was an Old English settlement, where the Anglo-Frisian dialect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> was commonly spoken in the south of the county until it declined in the mid-19th century. The town received its first charter in 1318, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> had a presence there.</p><p>In the 1640s the county strongly supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a>, and a fleet of Confederate privateers, with sailors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as well as local men, was based at Wexford and raided English Parliamentarian shipping. For this the town was sacked in 1649 during the Cromwellian conquest; many inhabitants were killed and much of the town was burned. In 1798 Wexford was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a> throughout the Wexford Rebellion, which brought a massacre of loyalists at nearby Scullabogue and executions and reprisals at Wexford Bridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wexford's port declined in the first half of the 20th century because the sands of the harbour kept shifting, and it closed to commercial shipping in 1968; a deepwater port at Rosslare Harbour, about 20 kilometres to the south, took the traffic. The town keeps Dutch Billy houses from the 18th century and twin churches with distinctive spires. It hosts the Wexford Opera Festival every October, begun by Dr Tom Walsh in 1951. The Wexford Wildfowl Reserve, a Ramsar site, shelters wintering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> white-fronted geese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wexford” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anglo-Norman knights led by Strongbow, who married Diarmait's daughter Aoife, took <b>Wexford</b>, Waterford and Dublin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Kingdom of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
